# Installed history acceptance client

This is an external acceptance driver, not product code or an installer. It calls
an already installed Control through its public HTTPS API, using the actual local
CA, normal CSRF/login cookies and the published audit API. It never receives a
PostgreSQL DSN and does not start, stop or seed a product or telemetry store.

## Build and client self-tests

Run `/root/lane-obs-tools/check-installed-history-driver.sh` on the Linux host.
It admits work only when both shared heavy locks, disk, free-memory and I/O
pressure guards allow it. Standard-library-only Go tests, vet and build run with
loopback networking only, dependency resolution disabled and two Go workers.
A successful client self-test is not an installed-product acceptance result.
The binary is `installed-history` next to `main.go`.

## Installed inputs

Copy `input.example.json` to an owner-only input file. Its placeholders deliberately
fail validation. Fill site/cell/Workspace from the actual installed Default scope;
seed raw and rollup samples with the matching labels and supply the exact UTC
whole-second windows and counts. A zero count is data. A missing point is absence.
Do not declare cells that the installation does not contain. Set sparse and zero
requirements only for the explicitly seeded acceptance data.

The Admin endpoint retains `https://admin.ownsolid.test:8443` as HTTP origin and TLS
identity. Choose exactly one dial override: an absolute `dial_unix_socket` for an
opaque TLS relay, or `dial_address` such as `127.0.0.1:8443` inside its namespace.
TLS is verified against `ca_file`; the driver never disables verification, follows
redirects, uses an ambient proxy or loads ambient trust roots. Raw and long origins
have their own CA references and no Admin cookie jar.

Input, password and saved session files must be absolute regular owner-only files
owned by the running account. Password files contain the exact password, optionally
followed by one line ending. Do not put credential contents in shell arguments or
logs. A pre-existing session file is never overwritten by init.

`source_sha` is a declared expectation, not proof of the installed binary. Record
the delivery manifest, image digest and actual Control BuildInfo for the same
frozen source before interpreting these results. The requested source is
`4463bbe628f60486bbdb92c15f72311fb53d076a`; older native fixtures remain separate.

## Phases

Run each command in the installation namespace, supplying the same protected input:

```sh
/path/to/installed-history -input /evidence/obs-history-input.json -mode init
/path/to/installed-history -input /evidence/obs-history-input.json -mode read
```

`read` verifies seeded raw and rollup points, a real raw-query positive control and
no raw fallback during rollup. Every successful history read must have exactly one
committed request-bound monitoring audit; refusals must have none. Supplying a
foreign Workspace is optional and must mean a different tenant, not another
Workspace administrable by this Platform Admin. It expects the actual public
`404 RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND` contract.

The installation owner performs each fault or restoration between these commands:

1. Stop only the owned long tier, run `long-unavailable`; restore it and run
   `long-restored`.
2. Serve the long tier with a certificate rejected by the configured CA or identity,
   run `long-tls-refused`; restore its valid certificate and run
   `long-tls-restored`. The direct long health failure must be an actual certificate
   verification failure, not a connection refusal. Close old proxy connections.
3. Disable the actual Workspace, run `workspace-disabled`; restore its active state
   and run `workspace-restored`. The original issued session is reused.
4. Run `logout-replay`. The client revokes its own session, replays the old issued
   cookies expecting 401 and no data, then logs in as the same actor and verifies
   recovery. No product or audit records are deleted.
5. Run `finish` after all configured required phases pass. It checks remaining audit
   expectations, logs out and removes only its own saved session file. On an
   incomplete/failed run, `abort` logs out and removes this validated own state
   without reporting the acceptance as passed.

Native `vm_http_requests_total{path="/api/v1/query_range"}` and
`process_start_time_seconds` are read from raw `/metrics`. Positive raw reads must
increment the counter. Rollup/refused reads must leave both counter and process
epoch unchanged; a reset or absent metric cannot prove no fallback. Store inspection
has no Admin cookies. JSON evidence includes request IDs, counts and timings only.

An optional `retention_window` requires five actual benchmark cells, ninety days of
explicitly declared synthetic recording output and its independent Workspace.
Only add `retention` to required phases when those inputs and resources exist.
It performs 31 public reads; timings include client validation and public audit
reads, retain host page cache, and do not represent production percentiles or
10,000 enrolled raw agents. Do not delay the installed main-flow gate for this
separate benchmark or invent a product topology for it.
